This problem is quite tricky!  First try and figure out which pair of angles are supplementary.  That means they add up to $180^\circ$.  After that figure out which pair of angles are complimentary to this pair, and then which pair of angles are vertical.  Keep in mind that there are 3 pairs of vertical angles, and thus there are 6 pairs of complementary angles.  There are 4 pairs of supplementary angles, and thus there are 6 pairs of non-adjacent interior angles.  There are 3 pairs of non-adjacent interior angles, and thus there are 3 pairs of angles that are both complementary and supplementary.  Since there are 6 pairs of complementary angles and 3 pairs of complementary and supplementary, there are 6/3=2 pairs of angles that are both complementary and supplementary.  Since two pairs of angles in a triangle sum to $180^\circ$, these must include all the angles of the triangle.  Thus the triangle's largest angle must be the one in each pair!  This is a fairly subtle problem involving some logic._gchandle
