One of the more important results in complex algebra is Descartes' rule of signs. This states that there are at most $r$ positive real zeros of a polynomial of degree $r$, and at most $r$ negative real zeros of that same polynomial. It also tells us that there is always at least one real root in the interval $[0,1]$, and there are at most two complex roots in the interval $[0,2 \pi)$.

We know that the complex roots in the interval $[0, 2 \pi)$ are the roots of the polynomial $z^8 - 1$ (since the polynomial is a factor of $z^8 - z^7 + z^6 - z^5 + z^4 - z^3 + z^2 - z + 1$). But the roots of $z^8 - 1$ are the $8$th roots of unity, so our roots are

\[\text{cis}\left( \frac{2k \pi}{8}\right) = \text{cis} \left( \frac{\pi k}{4}\right),\]for $k = 0, 1, 2, \dots, 7.$

We can see that $0 \le \frac{\pi k}{4} < 2 \pi$ for all of these, so the sum of all possible values of $\theta$ is

\[\frac{0\pi}{4} + \frac{1\pi}{4} + \frac{2\pi}{4} + \dots + \frac{7\pi}{4}\]

